--TEST--
An `autoload` declared in the entry point itself, after the code that needs it, resolves a class no `require` names — `rule:programs/autoload`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:programs/autoload-find-a-class-by-its-namespace
// `rule:programs/autoload`'s map is read by `nvs_hir` while the require/autoload graph is
// being built, which is before any code runs — so *where* the declaration is
// written cannot matter, and the entry point is a file like any other. The
// two facts this pins are both invisible from the bootstrap-file spelling the
// neighbouring cases use: the entry point may carry its own map, and the
// declaration is not a statement, so it is already in effect on the line
// above it. Nothing here names `src/Greeter.nvs` or `src/Text/Shout.nvs`.
echo App\Greeter::hello("world"), "\n";
echo App\Text\Shout::at("quiet"), "\n";

// Written last on purpose. A statement here would have run too late.
autoload 'App' from './src';
--FILE src/Greeter.nvs--
<?nvs
namespace App;

class Greeter
{
    public static function hello(string $who): string
    {
        return "hello, " . $who;
    }
}
--FILE src/Text/Shout.nvs--
<?nvs
namespace App\Text;

class Shout
{
    public static function at(string $what): string
    {
        return $what . "!";
    }
}
--EXPECT--
hello, world
quiet!
